2030-31.pdf","committee":"Council","date":"24/02/2026","key":true}],"read_documents":[],"calendar":{"upcoming":[{"date":"2026-09-24","committee":"Audit and Governance Committee","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.reading.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-13","committee":"Council","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.reading.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-19","committee":"Policy Committee","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.reading.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-21","committee":"Standing Advisory Council on Religious Education","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.reading.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-16","committee":"Policy Committee","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.reading.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-26","committee":"Audit and Governance Committee","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.reading.gov.uk"}],"recent":[{"date":"2026-06-23","committee":"Deadline for receipt of Questions and Petitions to Council - 12 noon","time":"12.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.reading.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-06-30","committee":"Council","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.reading.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-07-13","committee":"Policy Committee","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.reading.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-07-15","committee":"Audit and Governance Committee","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.reading.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-16","committee":"Policy Committee","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.reading.gov.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"KPMG has categorised Reading as Category A - audits expected to be relatively straightforward and able to sustain an ISA-compliant approach without significant difficulty.","lead":"For a lender, Category A classification is material positive news showing that the audit and financial control issues are not systemic or intractable, and the council has a clear, achievable pathway to restored audit credibility without major operational\u2026","iso":"2026-09-24","paper_title":"App 2 - 2025-26 Rebuilding assurance update Report _Sept 26","paper_url":"https://democracy.reading.gov.uk/documents/s41989/41989.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Four years of disclaimed audit opinions covering 2021/22 through 2024/25: predecessor auditor disclaimed 2021/22-2022/23, KPMG disclaimed 2023/24 and 2024/25.","lead":"Multiple years of disclaimed audit opinions signal governance or financial control weaknesses significant enough to prevent standard audit certification, a material concern for any lender assessing the council's creditworthiness and financial control\u2026","iso":"2026-09-24","paper_title":"App 2 - 2025-26 Rebuilding assurance update Report _Sept 26","paper_url":"https://democracy.reading.gov.uk/documents/s41989/41989.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"New special school provision of 244 places opening September 2027 to address SEND capacity shortfall","lead":"Capital investment to increase in-borough SEND provision may reduce reliance on expensive out-of-borough placements, potentially moderating the pace of DSG deficit growth, though complete mitigation of the \u00a350-97 million shortfall will require additional\u2026","iso":"2026-09-24","paper_title":"Strategic Risk Register September 2026 Final","paper_url":"https://democracy.reading.gov.uk/documents/s41983/41983.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Audit and Governance Committee","paper_title":"App 2 - 2025-26 Rebuilding assurance update Report _Sept 26","paper_url":"https://democracy.reading.gov.uk/documents/s41989/41989.pdf","headline":"KPMG has categorised Reading as Category A - audits expected to be relatively straightforward and able to sustain an ISA-compliant approach without significant difficulty.","detail":"Reading received the highest capacity assessment rating from MHCLG (Category A of four levels: A, B, C, D). This classification indicates the audit recovery is manageable within a defined timeline. The audit plan shows statutory reserves expected to return to unmodified opinion in 2025/26, with all other audit areas by 2026/27 or 2027/28.","category":"audit","matters":"For a lender, Category A classification is material positive news showing that the audit and financial control issues are not systemic or intractable, and the council has a clear, achievable pathway to restored audit credibility without major operational impediments.","figures":"Category A of four capacity levels; recovery timeline\u2014statutory reserves 2025/26, other audit areas 2026/27-2027/28","quote":"We have categorised the Reading Borough Council Audit as A","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Audit and Governance Committee","paper_title":"App 2 - 2025-26 Rebuilding assurance update Report _Sept 26","paper_url":"https://democracy.reading.gov.uk/documents/s41989/41989.pdf","headline":"Four years of disclaimed audit opinions covering 2021/22 through 2024/25: predecessor auditor disclaimed 2021/22-2022/23, KPMG disclaimed 2023/24 and 2024/25.","detail":"The predecessor auditor issued disclaimers for two years up to 2022/23. KPMG issued a disclaimer for 2023/24 on 28 February 2025 (statutory backstop date) and for 2024/25 on 27 February 2026. This extended period of disclaimed opinions indicates unresolved audit concerns affecting the reliability of the council's financial statements.","category":"audit","matters":"Multiple years of disclaimed audit opinions signal governance or financial control weaknesses significant enough to prevent standard audit certification, a material concern for any lender assessing the council's creditworthiness and financial control environment.","figures":"Four years: 2021/22 and 2022/23 (predecessor auditor); 2023/24 and 2024/25 (KPMG); backstop dates 28 February 2025 and 27 February 2026","quote":"For Reading Borough Council this had the impact of disclaimers of opinion issued by your predecessor auditor for 2 financial years up to and including 2022/23. We then issued a disclaimer of opinion for 2023/24 on 28 February 2025 to comply with the statutory backstop date as reported to you previously.  For the 2024/25 audit we issued a disclaimer of opinion on 27 February 2026.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Audit and Governance Committee","paper_title":"Strategic Risk Register September 2026 Final","paper_url":"https://democracy.reading.gov.uk/documents/s41983/41983.pdf","headline":"New special school provision of 244 places opening September 2027 to address SEND capacity shortfall","detail":"Building work is progressing on two school sites to deliver new special school provision of 244 places, scheduled for opening 01/09/2027. This capital investment directly addresses the gap between 1,184 places needed for children with EHCPs and 940 currently available. However, the risk score remains at 12 despite this progress, reflecting persistent underlying financial pressure from the High Needs Block deficit.","category":"capital","matters":"Capital investment to increase in-borough SEND provision may reduce reliance on expensive out-of-borough placements, potentially moderating the pace of DSG deficit growth, though complete mitigation of the \u00a350-97 million shortfall will require additional demand management.","figures":"244 new places; opening 01/09/2027; building progressing well as of July 2026; existing shortfall 1,184 needed vs 940 available","quote":"As of July 2026, building work is progressing well on the identified two school sites for the key action, new special school provision.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Audit and Governance Committee","paper_title":"Strategic Risk Register September 2026 Final","paper_url":"https://democracy.reading.gov.uk/documents/s41983/41983.pdf","headline":"Adult Social Care compliance with Care Act duties improved; waiting time reductions underway","detail":"The risk score for failure to meet Care Act statutory duties improved in Q2 2026-27, falling from 12 to 8 due to reduction in likelihood of breach. The paper cites operational improvement: longest overdue reviews reduced by one year. This improvement occurred despite sustained high demand of approximately 20 referrals per day, demonstrating effective performance management against a statutory safeguarding obligation.","category":"governance","matters":"Improvement in statutory compliance reduces legal and reputational liability and demonstrates effective operational management of demand under pressure, a positive indicator of financial and operational control.","figures":"Risk score improved 12 to 8 Q1-Q2 2026-27; longest overdue reviews reduced by one year; 20 referrals per day demand","quote":"Longest overdue reviews reduced by one year.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Audit and Governance Committee","paper_title":"Strategic Risk Register September 2026 Final","paper_url":"https://democracy.reading.gov.uk/documents/s41983/41983.pdf","headline":"Cyber risk remains at highest recorded level with no improvement expected; key mitigation delayed to 2027","detail":"The Council's cyber risk score remains at 20 (4 impact \u00d7 5 likelihood) with expected score unchanged, placing it among the highest risks in the register. Despite 75% email interception and blocking 5,000+ monthly malicious site access attempts, the threat environment continues evolving. Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) implementation is not yet started and scheduled for 31/09/2027. Cyber Essentials Plus certification, originally targeted March 2027, is delayed due to legacy application replacement issues.","category":"other","matters":"A sustained high-level cyber risk with no trajectory for improvement threatens the Council's operational continuity and ability to deliver services, impacting creditworthiness through both direct recovery costs and reputational damage.","figures":"Residual risk score 20 (unchanged); 75% of emails intercepted; 5,000+ monthly malicious link blocks; SIEM implementation scheduled 31/09/2027","quote":"The continuing evolution of the threat environment - not least owing to the war in Ukraine and Middle East - means that the likelihood will remain high, notwithstanding the significant set of actions in place.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-16"},"name":"Reading UA","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-19","since_iso":"2026-08-20","distress":false,"headline":"Government is considering a national Council Tax Support scheme redesign that could supersede this local scheme","lead":"A national scheme change could force the council to abandon this implementation, wasting project costs and creating timing uncertainty around a major revenue collection system","also":["19 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Audit and Governance Committee on 2026-09-24","n_findings":20,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"Council Tax Support Scheme","top_paper_url":"https://democracy.reading.gov.uk/documents/s41059/41059.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":151,"news_drafts_total":3}}